Remote work's impact on employee well-being presents a nuanced picture with both positive and negative dimensions. On the positive side, elimination of commuting stress has been consistently identified as a major benefit (Barrero et al., 2021). The average American commuter saves approximately 54 minutes daily when working remotely, time that can be redirected toward personal activities, family, or additional rest.Flexibility in work scheduling has emerged as another significant well-being benefit. Remote workers report greater ability to manage personal responsibilities and maintain better work-life integration (Brynjolfsson et al., 2020). Parents, in particular, benefit from the ability to be present for children's needs while maintaining professional responsibilities.Despite these benefits, remote work has introduced new stressors. Social isolation represents one of the most significant challenges, with many remote workers reporting feelings of disconnection from colleagues and organizational culture (Emanuel & Harrington, 2021). The absence of informal workplace interactions  —often called "water cooler conversations"  —has been linked to decreased job satisfaction and reduced sense of belonging.Boundary management between work and personal life has proven problematic for many remote workers. While flexibility is generally beneficial, the lack of physical separation between work and home spaces can lead to extended working hours and difficulty "switching off" from work responsibilities (Gibbs et al., 2021).Longitudinal studies suggest that the well-being impacts of remote work may evolve over time. Initial improvements in work-life balance may diminish as remote workers struggle with isolation and career development concerns (Barrero et al., 2021). Professional development and mentoring relationships, traditionally facilitated through in-person interactions, face particular challenges in remote environments.





