{"query":"Compare weakly supervised learning vs self supervised learning applied to computer vision, specifically in video activity recognition or classification.","report":"
Video activity recognition, also known as action recognition, has made significant progress with deep learning, but it traditionally requires large amounts of labeled video data. Annotating videos is labor-intensive and costly, especially when precise temporal information is needed for each action [6]. Moreover, models trained purely on human-labeled data can inherit labeling biases and may generalize poorly across domains [6]. To mitigate the reliance on extensive annotated datasets, researchers have explored *weakly supervised learning* and *self-supervised learning* for video understanding. Both paradigms aim to leverage unlabeled or partially labeled data to learn effective video representations, thereby reducing the need for full supervision. According to one explanation, weakly supervised learning means the model “learns from partially labeled or noisy data” [5] – for example, learning from coarse video-level tags or incomplete annotations. In contrast, self-supervised learning relies on *no explicit labels at all*, instead **learning from the data itself** by predicting parts of the input (such as future frames or context) as a surrogate objective [5]. These approaches have gained traction in computer vision as ways to harness large-scale video data without dense labeling.

Researchers have broadly categorized self-supervised video learning methods into several families based on learning objectives [6]. Common categories include *pretext tasks* (artificial tasks like shuffling frames or solving puzzles for representation learning), *generative methods* (predicting or reconstructing video content), *contrastive methods* (distinguishing between different clips or contexts), and *cross-modal methods* (learning from multiple modalities, such as audio-visual correspondence) [6]. Weakly supervised approaches, on the other hand, often leverage *incomplete or inexact annotations*, such as video-level labels without temporal boundaries, to guide learning. In the following sections, we compare weakly supervised and self-supervised learning in the context of video activity recognition. We discuss representative techniques of each, highlight key research works, and analyze their relative strengths and challenges for classifying or recognizing actions in videos."}

